====================================================================================================
(A) Repeated sample: summable priors end at maximum likelihood; a non-summable prior need not
====================================================================================================
(A1) random finite classes, samples with (a),(b): 1074; at r = floor(r_ML)+1 every w-MAP attains P*: 1074 of 1074; largest r_ML = 102227.4
(A2) realisation: theta_j with 4 KL(1/4||theta_j) = 1/j^3 bits: j=1: 0.539296, j=2: 0.346426, j=5: 0.273249, j=10: 0.258118, j=100: 0.250255
     non-summable prior log2 w(pi*) = -1, log2 w(pi_j) = 1/j; on h^r the score of pi_j is 1/j - r/j^3 (relative to r log P*)
   r =         1: unique MAP pi_j at j =     2 (sqrt(3r) =       1.7), score 3.750e-01 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 4 scores below 1/4; maximisers: 1
   r =        10: unique MAP pi_j at j =     6 (sqrt(3r) =       5.5), score 1.204e-01 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 10 scores below 1/10; maximisers: 1
   r =       100: unique MAP pi_j at j =    17 (sqrt(3r) =      17.3), score 3.847e-02 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 27 scores below 1/27; maximisers: 1
   r =     10000: unique MAP pi_j at j =   173 (sqrt(3r) =     173.2), score 3.849e-03 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 261 scores below 1/261; maximisers: 1
   r =   1000000: unique MAP pi_j at j =  1732 (sqrt(3r) =    1732.1), score 3.849e-04 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 2600 scores below 1/2600; maximisers: 1
   r = 100000000: unique MAP pi_j at j = 17321 (sqrt(3r) =   17320.5), score 3.849e-05 > 0 > -1 = score of pi*; tail j >= 25982 scores below 1/25982; maximisers: 1
   So at every r the MAP exists and has likelihood P* 2^(-1/j^3) < P*: summability cannot be dropped in Prop 2.2(iv).

====================================================================================================
(B) Which fixed priors reproduce the cn-MAP sets on all samples of size <= N?
====================================================================================================
(B1) coin (length 1) / deterministic f (length 1+10), all samples of size n <= 30 (only n and f-consistency matter)
   c = 1/20 (ck = 1/2): priors (1,t) consistent with all samples n <= 30: log2 t in (-1, inf); degenerate priors matching: []
   c = 1/5 (ck = 2): priors (1,t) consistent with all samples n <= 30: log2 t in (-inf, -30); degenerate priors matching: ['(1,0)']
   ck = 1/2: every full-support prior with t > 1/2 works (no sample has (a),(b)); ck = 2: only t < 2^-30 survives n <= 30,
   so in the limit only delta_coin (no full support) reproduces the cn-MAPs: the hypothesis of Prop 2.2(ii) is needed.

(B2) two Bernoulli rates: pi_h = rate 1/2 (length 1), pi_q = rate 1/4 (length 1+k), k = 10; log2(3/2) = 0.584963
   c = 0.01    (ck = 0.100, ck < log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: none (empty from N = 12 on); degenerate priors matching: none
   c = 0.03    (ck = 0.300, ck < log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: none (empty from N = 4 on); degenerate priors matching: none
   c = 0.05    (ck = 0.500, ck < log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: none (empty from N = 4 on); degenerate priors matching: none
   c = 0.0584  (ck = 0.584, ck < log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: none (empty from N = 4 on); degenerate priors matching: none
   c = 0.0586  (ck = 0.586, ck > log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: log2 t in (-inf, -23.399); degenerate priors matching: ['(1,0)']
   c = 0.1     (ck = 1.000, ck > log(3/2)): (1,t) feasible for n <= 40: log2 t in (-inf, -23.399); degenerate priors matching: ['(1,0)']
   smallest |g(phat) - ck| over all samples examined: 4.821e-04 (no float tie)
   For ck < log(3/2): no fixed prior of any kind reproduces the cn-MAP sets (Prop 2.2(v)); for ck > log(3/2) delta_{pi_h} does.

